## INTRODUCTION
With burgeoning demands for multifunctionalities and hierarchical structures in applications ranging from catalysis to drug delivery, research on multi-component materials with hierarchically arranged functionality has attracted increased attention recently.1-4 Reticular chemistry, which emphasizes constructing framework materials by linking molecular building units through strong bonds, presents as an ideal platform with highly tunable hierarchy and diversity.5-8 In particular, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are two well-developed classes of porous materials linked via molecular building units, which present fascinating advances in fields including separation, heterogeneous catalysis, and controlled delivery.9-16
Efforts have been made to fabricate multi-component hierarchical MOFs with controlled compositions and distributions of building units, bringing about cooperative effects among multiple components for specific targets.17-27 For example, the epitaxial growth method (EGM) has succeeded in some cases of MOF-on-MOF architectures.21, 24 In addition, Kitagawa and coworkers introduced a polymer to assemble two MOF phases with distinct topologies into one composite.28 Zhou group recently reported the synthesis of a series of hybrid core–shell MOFs with mismatched lattice parameters through kinetic control.29 The synthetic applicability of the hierarchical MOFs was extended to varying metal clusters and organic linkers under the guidance of retrosynthetic analysis.30 Additionally, Rosi and coworkers also developed a “domain building block” approach to diversify the stratified MOFs through epitaxial growth.31 The individual component of these hierarchical MOFs could further serve as programmable modules allowing for the investigation into hierarchy and functions.32
To bridge metal- and covalent organic frameworks hierarchically in reticular chemistry, some preliminary studies have been conducted in recent years, which indicate the feasibility of integrating MOFs and COFs. Introducing dynamic covalent chemistry into MOF chemistry could generate a series of structures with COF units, for example, MOF-901(Ti) based on imine bonds33, PCN-16X $\mathrm{(X=1-4,}$ )
based on imine bonds25, CPM-100 based on boron–oxygen bonds34. Another approach to integrate MOFs and COFs is to fabricate hierarchical hybrid composites. In 2018, Zhang and coworkers utilized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68(\mathrm{In})$ modified with tris( $\dot{.}4$ -formylphenyl)amine (TFPA) as a core for the growth of TPA-COF as a shell to generate a core-shell composite.35 Lan and coworkers anchored $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 onto TpPa-1- COF through the connection of imine bonds and studied their performance over photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution.36 Kim group also prepared a Pd doped $\mathrm{MIL-125^{-}N H_{2}(T i)}@\mathrm{LZU-1}$ core-shell structure via interfacial growth.37 Very recently, Imaz, Maspoch and coworkers prepared $\mathrm{MOF@COF}$ composites synthesized through two steps including spray‐dry‐ ing and amorphous‐to‐crystalline transformation.38 The resulting composites exhibited superior porosity due to the presence of micro‐ and mesopores at the MOF/COF inter‐ face.
Although some progresses have been made, there is a lack of systematic studies over hierarchical metal- and covalent organic framework composites. Crucially, there is no report on the preparation of COF@MOF hybrid composites while some MOF@COF composites have been prepared and studied. Moreover, precise control over spatial distributions, compositions, and crystal sizes has never been achieved in previous hierarchical systems. Herein, we report an approach of modular total synthesis to bridge metal- and covalent organic frameworks hierarchically in reticular chemistry. The idea of modularity in total synthesis involves disconnecting an integrated system into smaller individual ‘modules’ which can be interchanged, while diverse structures can be achieved by simply varying the building blocks (Scheme 1). In other words, modular total synthesis can be viewed as a convergent bottom-up approach to generate targeted structures with architectural intricacy from sequence-defined reactions between diverse building blocks. As a proof of concept, the controlled generation of hierarchical two-module and threemodule COF-on-MOF architectures was performed. Accordingly, the tunability of spatial apportionment and compositions have been achieved in these modular systems. This general synthetic approach shall not only accelerate the discovery of multi-component porous materials by assembling MOFs and COFs hierarchically, but also offer a facile route to design smart materials with unlimited tunability, synergism, and precision owing to the high designability and diversity of these inorganic or organic building units.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis, stability and alignment studies of COF${\bf303}$ microrods. We first chose COF-303 as a module platform to support the secondary growth of shell MOFs. COF303 is constructed through a reversable imine condensation between a tetratopic aldehyde building block, tetrakis( $\overline{{4}}$ -formylphenyl)methane (TFM), and a ditopic amine, phenylenediamine (PDA).39 The single crystals of COF-303 can be obtained via an imine-exchange approach with a large excess of aniline presented (Figure 1a). After two weeks of nucleation and growth, needle-like COF-303 single crystals with lengths of around $15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were obtained (Figure S1).
To allow the formation of a second module outside the first module COF-303, the stability is fatal for the consideration to avoid the unwanted decomposition of COF-303 under the solvothermal condition. In order to confirm the stability of COF-303, the crystals were immersed into various solvent environments for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ , including organic solvents and aqueous solutions with varying $\mathsf{p H}$ values. As indicated by the optical images and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure $S2-3=$ ), the COF-303 constructed from covalent bonds was robust enough to maintain the structural integrity under harsh conditions.
Interestingly, these DMF-suspended COF-303 microrod crystals could be dynamically aligned by shaking the holder under the influence of gravity field. When COF-303 microrods were horizontally placed on a holder, they tended to form randomly oriented patterns as indicated by the optical images and the corresponding polarized optical images (Figure S1). In contrast, when the sample holder was inclined, COF-303 microrods were well-aligned, which were parallel to the flow direction of DMF solution, due to the particle shape anisotropy of COF microrods (Figure S1). The alignment and relaxation of COF-303 microrods in the flow could be reversibly tuned by simply rotating the sample holder. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report on the COF alignment, and shall open up new opportunities for developing switchable COF assemblies and devices under various fields including gravitational, electric and magnetic fields.
Modular synthesis of hierarchical COF@MOF composites. Given the excellent stability of COF-303 under various conditions, the COF-303 single crystals were used as a scaffold for the preparation of hierarchical COF$303@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ composites because of the mild synthetic conditions of Zn-based MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ (Figure $\mathbf{S_{4}}$ ). The crystals of COF-303 were immersed into a DEF solution of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As expected, a multicomponent hierarchical COF- $-303@\mathrm{MOF}.$ - 5 was obtained, which could be clearly distinguished via the optical micrsocopy (Figure 1b-d, $\mathbf{\DeltaS}_{5}\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}$ ). Polarized optical images of $\mathrm{COF-}303@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ placed in between crossed polarizers also displayed a clear well-dispersed COF-on-MOF pattern under a polarized light (Figure 1e-f). The core COF-303, constructed from imine linkages, exhibited as yellow needle-like crystals, whereas the shell MOF- $5$ displayed a colorless cubic morphology. The well-maintained crystallinity of both core COF-303 and shell $\mathrm{\bfMOF-}5$ were demonstrated by the PXRD patterns of the hierarchical COF@MOF structures (Figure 2b). Due to the labile nature of $\mathrm{\bfMOF-}5.$ , the shell of the composites could be selectively removed under mild acid treatment, while the crystallinity and morphology of released core COF-303 remained unaltered (Figure S6).
The $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherm of the hierarchical COF-onMOF composites was further studied (Figure 2a, Table S1). As indicated from $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms, COF-303@MOF5 displayed a slightly lower $\Nu_{2}$ uptake as compared with its single MOF module, due to the presence of core COF crystals with much lower $\Nu_{2}$ uptake in the hierarchical composite. The microporous nature of this composite indicated the intact interfaces between the COF module and MOF module, which was consistent with the SEM analysis of $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composites. This is quite different from the previous report by Imaz, Maspoch and coworkers, where MOF@COF composites exhibited superior porosity due to the presence of micro‐ and mesopores at the MOF/COF in‐ terface.38 The fast crystallization during spray‐drying process creates more defects at the MOF/COF interface, while the solvothermal conditions in this report allow slow secondary growth of MOFs outside COF microrods, limiting the formation of defects at a mesoscopic level.
Interestingly, we found that the addition of COF seeds into MOF precursors greatly influenced the nucleation and growth of MOF- $5$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) revealed that the COF-303@MOF-5 exhibited a cubic morphology with ordered lamellar surface while COF-303 microrods were dispersed on $\mathrm{\bfMOF-}5$ (Figure 2c-f, ${\bf{S7^{-9}}}.$ ). This unprecedented one-pot generation of fractal $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composites provides a facile route to fabricate hierarchical architectures.
It was observed that the morphology of COF-303 microrods was well maintained in the hierarchical structure, indicating its stability during the growth of MOF- $5$ . The formation of this fractal $\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ superstructures with orderly aligned protuberant stripes on the surface was rarely observed in literatures, although there are several recent studies on HKUST-1 with similar stacked-nanosheet morphology as reported.40-41 Based on the reported literatures and observations in our studies, we propose that there exist imine fragments in defective sites both on the COF surfaces and inside COFs. These imine fragments could decompose partially during the secondary growth of MOF- $5$ which released amine and aldehyde into the solution phase.
These released fragments would further interfere with the nucleation and modulate the growth of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF- $5$ .
Tuning compositions, apportionment and diversity in COF@MOF composites.  Next, we demonstrated that the COF-303 to $\mathrm{\bfMOF-}5$ ratio could be adjusted in the hierarchical ${\mathrm{COF@MOF}}$ composites by simply controlling the feed ratios of the two modules. The optical images and polarized optical images of the resulting COF-303@MOF-5 with varying COF amounts were shown in Figure 3a. In addition, the apportionment of core COFs in shell MOF modules could be contolled, resulting in well-mixed or Janus distribution (Figure 3b). In this case, COF microrods were found to be well stabilized at the vial bottom through centrifugal precipitation. This feature further allowed the anisotropic growth of MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ crystals, giving rise to a Janus crystal of $\mathrm{COF-}303@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5.$ , instead of well-mixed distribution in the normal synthesis (Figure 1d). As indicated by optical images under a polarized light, the asymmetric distribution of COF microrods could be clearly distinguished (Figure $\mathbf{3^{b}}$ ).
To further examine whether modular total synthesis could guide the synthesis of porous framework materials in reticular chemistry, we studied the sequence-defined combination of a series of classical MOFs and COFs (Figure 4). One key factor in modular total synthesis is to determine the sequence of covalent or coordination bond strengths, which ensures the structural integrity of core modules during the assembly of shell modules (Figure 4a). We firstly investigated the stability of COF-303 microrods in varying MOF synthetic conditions. For example, COF303 microrods were immersed in the acidic solution of UiO-67 precursors containing $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ , 4, $4^{'}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid (BPDC) linkers and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) as a modulator. It was found that the COF microrods were robust enough to survive in the harsh MOF synthetic conditions, as indicated by the optical images after $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure $\mathbf{S2}$ ). This stability trend is also consistent with the bond strength tendency, where dynamic covalent bonds are stronger than coordination bonds (Figure 4a). Therefore, we chose MOFs, for example, functionalized MOF- $5$ - Br, interpenetrated MOF- $5$ -BPDC, MOF-177, ZIF-8, UiO-67 and PCN-160 as shell MOFs, while more robust COFs including COF-303 and COF-300 were used as cores. After modular synthesis, a series of COF@MOF composite crystals including COF-303@MOF-177, COF-303@MOF- $5$ - Br and COF-303@MOF- $5$ -BPDC, COF-303@ZIF-8, COF303@UiO-67, COF-303@PCN-160  and COF-300@MOF-5 were obtained, as indicated by the optical and SEM images (Figure 4b-g, S13-25). Our modular synthesis here exhibits a general and powerful method to fabricate hierarchical COF-on-MOF structures with architectural intricacy.
Diversifying porous multi-component materials with hierarchical structures. Furthermore, we shown that $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composites can be modified into sophisticated framework materials with tailored hierarchy and enhanced diversity. As a proof of concept, a two-module ${\mathrm{COF@MOF}}$ was initially prepared. Subsequent click reactions and acid treatments were utilized to selectively transform the MOF module into a polymer, while the COF module remained in its original state. This modular modification leads to the formation of a $\mathrm{COF}@$ polymer composite, while it can be conducted independently within each module, without affecting the status of other modules.
A hierarchical COF-303@Zn-AzTPDC was prepared as a scaffold for the fabrication of a $\mathrm{COF}@$ polymer composite (AzTPDC stands for diazide-triphenyldicarboxylate, Figure 5). The azide groups on the Zn-AzTPDC framework could undergo a copper-catalyzed azide–alkyne cycloaddition click reaction with tetra-acetylene cross-linker (CL4, Figure 5).32, $^{42}$ After polymerization, COF-300 microrods inside interconnected Zn-AzTPDC-CL4 were obtained (Figure 5). Subsequently, the polymerized COF-303@ZnAzTPDC-CL4 was immersed into a HCl/dimethylformamide (DMF) $(\mathbf{1}/5,\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ solution. Considering the differences in chemical stability of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs and COFs, the Zn clusters in polymerized Zn-AzTPDC-CL4 could be selectively removed, while the structure of more stable COF-303 microrods could be maintained, as indicated by the optical images (Figure 5). As a result, the hierarchical $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composites could be transformed into COF@polymer composites, which contain both crystalline and amorphous organic polymers, without a remarkable change in the morphology. Notably, without the clicked polymerization, COF-303@Zn-AzTPDC could not maintain its cubic morphology after acid treatment: the shell Zn-AzTPDC would gradually dissolve, releasing core COF-303 microrods during the decomposition process (Figure S26).
To further demonstrate the concept of modularity in the synthesis of hierarchical reticular compounds, we prepared an integrated three-module system with an internal stability sequence (Figure 6, S27-28). To coat a third module with different components outside the two-module $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composites, we chose a relatively labile Znbased $\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ as the outer module, while a relatively robust COF-303@PCN-160 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ as the inner module. The colorless feature of $\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ and orange feature of PCN-160 could be easily distinguished by optical images, while the COF microrods were visible under polarized lights (Figure 6). This synthetic route was conducted based on the modular synthesis principles of hierarchical reticular compounds, in which the sequence is in the order of decreasing bond strengths of these three modules $(\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{N}>\mathsf{Z r}{\mathsf{-O}}>\mathsf{Z n}{\mathsf{-O}})$ . As a result, multiple synthetic sequences were achieved from COF-303 to $\mathrm{COF}{-}303@\mathrm{PCN}{-}160$ , COF-303@PCN-160-BDC, and $(\mathrm{COF-303}@\mathrm{PCN-1}60)@\mathrm{MOF-}5$ . The successful fabrication of the three-module $(\mathrm{COF@MOF})@\mathrm{MOF}$ utilizes COF-303@ PCN-160 as a seed, which allows for the epitaxial growth of ${\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5;$ , as indicated by the optical images (Figure 6). After the subsequent growth of MOF-5, the morphology and color of the original $\mathrm{COF}{-}303@\mathrm{PCN}{-}160$ were maintained, indicating the power of modular synthesis in the hierarchical integration of multiple modules (Figure 6).
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we successfully demonstrated the application of modularity in reticular chemistry, which enables the fabrication of hierarchically arranged framework materials, including MOFs, COFs and porous polymers. The structural, compositional, and functional tunability of the hierarchical material system can be exponentially enhanced by independently modifying each module. Guided by the assembly sequence, which is determined by the coordination or covalent bond strengths, the modular synthesis progressively connects simple building blocks into increasingly sophisticated superstructures. Considering the expanding applications of modularity in organic total synthesis, we envision that this concept shall also accelerate the discovery of multi-component framework materials in reticular chemistry, where the hierarchically arranged functionality is promising for applications including cooperative catalysis, efficient guest delivery, cascade separation and storage, and so on.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
$\mathbf{N_{2}}$ sorption isotherms. Gas sorption measurements were conducted using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 system. Prior to gas sorption measurements, about 50-100 mg samples were washed thoroughly with fresh DMF and exchanged by chloroform for $^3$ times, during which the solvent was decanted and freshly replenished three times every day. The solvent was then removed under vacuum at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h, yielding the porous material.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). PXRD was carried out with a Bruker D8-Focus Bragg-Brentano X-ray Powder Diffractometer equipped with a Cu sealed tube $\langle\lambda=1.54178$ Å) at 40 kV and $40~\mathrm{{mA}}$ .
1H NMR spectroscopy. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) data were collected on an Inova 500 spectrometer.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Images and analyses of SEM/EDX were taken by FEI Quanta 600 FESEM. The Quanta 600 FEG is a field emission scanning electron microscope capable of generating and collecting high-resolution and low-vacuum images. It is equipped with a motorized x-y-z-tilt-rotate stage, providing the following movements: $\mathrm{x}=\mathrm{y}=150\ \mathrm{mm}$ (motorized); $\mathbf{Z}=65\mathrm{{mm}}$ (motorized); Tilt $+70$ degrees to $^{-5}$ degrees (motorized); Source: Field emission gun assembly with Schottky emitter source. Voltage: 200 $\mathrm{v}$ to 30 kV. Beam Current: >100 nA.
Stability test of single-crystalline COF-303. The single crystals of COF-303 were synthesized using an imineexchange approach with a large excess of aniline presented, based on the previous report.39 About 5 mg COF-303 crystals were immersed into various solvent environments for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ , including organic solvents and aqueous solutions with varying $\mathsf{p H}$ values.
Synthesis of COF-303@MOF- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf5}$ . COF-303 $\left(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{3}\right.$ or $5~\mathrm{mg}$ , respectively), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $\left(8.8~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ , $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $\left(41.6~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and DEF $(_{2}\mathrm{mL})$ were charged in a Pyrex vial. The mixture was heated in an $85^{\circ}C$ oven for $_{24}\mathrm{h}$ to generate the crystals of $\mathrm{COF-}303@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ . The crystals were collected by centrifugation and washed with fresh DMF for 3 times. Various amounts of COF-303 can be immobilized into $\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ by tuning the initial amount during modular synthesis.
Synthesis of COF-303@Zn-AzTPDC. COF-303 $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ , $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ (57 mg), AzTPDC $\left(28\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and DEF $(5.0\mathrm{mL})$ were charged in a Pyrex vial. The mixture was heated in an 80 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\mathsf{o}}\mathsf{C}$ oven for $^3$ days to generate the crystals of COF$303@Z\mathrm{n-AzTPDC}$ . The solution was decanted, and the crystals were repeatedly washed with fresh DMF for 3 times.
Synthesis of polymerized $\mathbf{coF}@$ polymer (COF303@poly-AzTPDC-CL4). COF-303@Zn-AzTPDC was immersed in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of 0.1 M CL4 solution (DMF) in a screw top vial, and ${\bf\varepsilon}_{250}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of saturated CuBr solution (DMF) was added to the mixture. The vial was kept standing at $80~^{\circ}C$ for 3 days. The supernatant was decanted, and the cubic crystals, $\mathrm{COF-303@2n-AzTPDC-CL4}.$ , were repeatedly washed with fresh DMF for 3 times. COF-303@ZnAzTPDC-CL4 was immersed in a mixed solvent of HCl/DMF (1:5, v/v) in a screw top vial. The vial was kept standing at room temperature for $_{3}\mathrm{h}$ . The supernatant was decanted, and cubic crystals were repeatedly washed with fresh DMF for 3 times.
Synthesis of COF-303@PCN-160. COF-303 $(_{3}~\mathrm{mg})$ , $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ (10 mg), AZDC $\mathrm{(10~mg)}$ ), TFA $\left(0.2{\mathrm{~mL}}\right)$ and DMF (1.5 mL) were charged in a Pyrex vial. The mixture was heated in 120 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\mathsf{o}}\mathsf{C}$ oven for 5 days. After cooling down to room temperature, the resulting $\mathrm{COF}{-}303@\mathrm{PCN}{-}160$ crystals were separated by centrifugation and washed with fresh DMF for $^3$ times.
Synthesis of $(\mathbf{COF-303@PCN-160})@\mathbf{MOF-5}$ . COF303@PCN-160 $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ , $_{\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}}$ $(8.8~\mathrm{mg})$ , DEF $(\mathbf{1}~\mathrm{mL})$ were charged in a Pyrex vial. The mixture was heated in an $85^{\circ}C$ oven for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ . $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $\left(41.6~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and DEF $\mathrm{\bf(1~mL)}$ were further added into the vial. The mixture was heated in an $85^{\circ}C$ oven for $_{24}\mathrm{h}$ to generate the crystals of (COF$303@\mathrm{PCN-160})@\mathrm{MOF-}5$ . The crystals were collected by centrifugation and washed with fresh DMF for $^3$ times.
